WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.478
JUAN SEBASTIAN OVIEDO: Hello.

00:00:01.478 --> 00:00:03.060
My name is Juan
Sebastian Oviedo.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.090
And I am a product manager
for continuous integration,

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.450
continuous delivery
on Google Cloud.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:12.180
Today, we'll be talking about an
exciting new feature, Container

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.520
Registry Vulnerability Scanning.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.440
Scanning your images for
known vulnerabilities

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.810
is an important step in
securing your CI/CD pipeline.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.620
Vulnerability scanning helps
you identify and address

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.630
vulnerabilities in images stored
in Google Container Registry.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:34.290
In turn, this enables you to
prioritize patching your images

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.630
to other [INAUDIBLE]
vulnerabilities,

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.970
determine whether your
environment is affected

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.820
by a newly discovered
vulnerability,

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.030
and ensure that your
images are up to date

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.790
and protected against
the latest threats.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.880
First, enable the
container and analysis

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.770
API to use the
vulnerability scanner.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.920
Once enabled, it
will automatically

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.660
scan the container
images you have

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.700
stored in container registry.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.400
It uses data from
vulnerability databases

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.290
for the supported distributions.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.480
After an image is
scanned, you can

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.580
see the list of vulnerabilities
found and their severity

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.910
via the Cloud console,
the command line

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:15.870
interface, and vulnerability
scanner's APIs.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.070
Also, the vulnerability status
of previously scanned images

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.770
is automatically updated
when new vulnerabilities

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.410
are reported to the
source databases.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.020
To learn more about a
specific vulnerability

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.130
in an image, click on it
to go to the detailed view.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.620
Using this information,
you can find and get

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.530
a patch version of the
effected container image.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.840
If no vulnerabilities
are found for an image,

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:44.800
it will have a green check with
none found in the vulnerability

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.110
column.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.030
You can apply
additional security

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.690
to your CI/CD process
with binary authorization.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.870
Set up policies to ensure that
only container images that

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:58.950
meet your acceptance criteria
can be used in deployments.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.590
So to recap,
vulnerability scanner

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.960
helps you secure
your CI/CD process

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.720
by continuously
identifying vulnerabilities

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.610
in your container images.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.650
You can use this
information to decide

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.750
what images need to be patched.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.760
To learn more, check
out documentation

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.520
for Container Registry
Vulnerability Scanning.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.480
Thanks for watching.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.230
[LOGO MUSIC PLAYING]

